{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011ChPtt9NJKJnnosq5xaEsG","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01QgHCGUChA1GDGKfPaTBqak","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /work; python3 - <<'EOF'\np='docs/proposal.md'\ns=open(p).read()\ndef rep(old,new,cnt=1):\n    global s\n    assert s.count(old)==cnt,(old,s.count(old))\n    s=s.replace(old,new)\n\n# flowchart\nrep('''    R -->|\"ard:review:ROLE:changes (max 3 rounds)\"| W\n''','''    R -->|\"ard:review:ROLE:changes (max 3 rounds)\"| W\n    W -->|\"needs-network: one round, first job only\"| T\n''')\nrep(\"A reviewer's `changes` verdict sends the PR back to the worker, at most three times;\",\n    \"A worker that answers `needs-network` before it has pushed anything goes back to the triage engineer once (the edge from Worker to Triage); a reviewer's `changes` verdict sends the PR back to the worker, at most three times;\")\n\n# line 134\nrep(\" For now the outcome ends like `needs-human`: nothing is pushed, no PR is opened, and the comment lists each entry with its URL in a code span. Part 2 (#385) adds the bounce to the triage engineer, and part 3 (#386) switches it on (`servecmd` sets `closeout.Deps.Network`) together with the lead re-arm and the inputs gate. When on, it applies to a first worker job only (no branch or PR, and nothing pushed by this job): close-out requests\",\n    \" The bounce is on in `serve` (`servecmd` sets `closeout.Deps.Network`) and applies to a first worker job only (no branch or PR, and nothing pushed by this job): nothing is pushed and no PR is opened, and close-out requests\")\nrep(\"a pushed job or a repository without a triage engineer role ends `needs-human` with the list. The live prompts under `.ard/` need a human PR to match the starter copies.\",\n    \"a pushed job or a repository without a triage engineer role ends `needs-human`, with one comment that lists each entry with its URL in a code span. The same applies when the bounce is off. The live prompts under `.ard/` need a human PR to match the starter copies; a test drives the whole path (bounce, re-run, gate, approval, worker, reviewers and the three negative cases) against fake agents with no network.\")\nrep(\"(the last three add ard:needs-human for now)\",\"(the last three add ard:needs-human, except a first needs-network, which bounces to triage)\")\n\n# label table\nrep(\"| Ready for triage; the lead's request label |\",\"| Ready for triage; the lead's request label. Also set by the engineer's close-out in a network round, to re-run the lead |\")\nrep(\"| This triage role is requested |\",\"| This triage role is requested. A needs-network worker close-out requests the engineer this way, and the lead's close-out re-requests the other roles in a network round |\")\nrep(\"`ard:pending` is removed when the lead finishes, so it means \\\"not yet triaged\\\" and nothing else.\",\n    \"`ard:pending` is removed when the lead finishes, so it means \\\"not yet triaged\\\" and nothing else. In a network round the bounce reverses part of that: the worker close-out removes `ard:triage:engineer:done`, `ard:worker:<role>` and `:running` and requests the engineer; the engineer's close-out removes `ard:triage:lead:done` and adds `ard:pending` before its own `:done`; the lead's close-out removes the `:done` of the roles it re-opens and requests them again. The gate does not run while the lead's `:done` is absent.\")\n\n# worker step 9\nrep(\" A role file that says `needs-network` ends like `needs-human` for now: nothing is pushed, and the comment lists the needed inputs.\",\n    \" A role file that says `needs-network` on a first job (no branch, no PR, nothing pushed) bounces the issue to the triage engineer once, as described under the role-file contract; in any other case it ends `needs-human`, nothing is pushed, and the comment lists the needed inputs.\")\nrep(\"with `Network: unavailable.` it answers `needs-human` listing the inputs it needs (#301).\",\n    \"with `Network: unavailable.` it fetches nothing and answers `needs-network` with a `network:` list (`what`, `url`, `why`, optional `verify`) when no branch exists yet, or `needs-human` when a branch or PR does (#301, #384). The controller bounces a first `needs-network` once; the second ends `needs-human`.\")\n\n# starter lead / engineer\nrep(\"reads the `Worker roles:` line to see whether any worker role has network (#301).\",\n    \"reads the `Worker roles:` line to see whether any worker role has network (#301). In a network round the controller re-runs it after the engineer, so it must route the same way again; it never sees the round otherwise.\")\nrep(\"it never fetches and never writes a checksum it did not read from a file (#301).\",\n    \"it never fetches and never writes a checksum it did not read from a file (#301). When a job has `network-request.md` (the untrusted, redacted list of what a worker asked for), it reads it, updates `### Network inputs` and declares `inputs:` in `triage-engineer.md` (`path`, `provider` `human` or `worker`, optional https `url`), at most 10 (#386).\")\n\n# gate\nrep(\"An approval from anyone else adds `ard:needs-human` with a comment that does not name the actor.\",\n    \"In a network round the gate first checks the engineer's `inputs:`: a human-provided path missing from `main` holds it with one \\\"waiting for inputs\\\" comment, and when the files land it posts a fresh \\\"ready for approval\\\" with a new `round=<n>` marker, even with `require_approval: false`; an earlier approval is stale because the engineer's new `:done` follows it, so a maintainer removes and re-adds `ard:approved`. A `worker` input with a `network: false` worker role, a bad path or an oversized file ends `needs-human`. An approval from anyone else adds `ard:needs-human` with a comment that does not name the actor.\")\n\n# configuration\nrep(\"In host mode the controller logs that `network: false` is not enforced.\",\n    \"In host mode the controller logs that `network: false` is not enforced. `network: false` on a worker is what makes it answer `needs-network`: the controller bounces that to the triage engineer once, and the gate refuses an input the engineer assigns to a `worker` provider when the worker role has `network: false` (the issue ends `needs-human`, so a human provides the file on `main`).\")\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\nEOF\ngo test ./internal/docscheck/... 2>&1 | tail -5"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1805,"cache_read_input_tokens":80924,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1805},"output_tokens":30,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"611ba83e-bb6c-42b4-b062-b83e0a9fbcfe","uuid":"6c36f5f3-7b82-49b6-8d33-7f019a2cb74b","timestamp":"2026-11-25T15:38:00.914Z","request_id":"req_011ChPtt8zDjFvofWSTihc6P"}
